Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 09:49:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 40/40] System.Web.DataVisualization: what comparing every API
 with .NET Framework's found

Every public member and twelve scenarios (every chart type drawn, keywords in three cultures, formulas,
statistics, data binding, serialization, image maps and formats) run on .NET Framework 4.8 and on the port
(the repository's tests/DataVisualizationParity), and a Web Forms site with ChartImg.axd on IIS and on the
port, on Windows and in a Linux container (samples/ChartProbe).

- Numbers as .NET Framework writes them (Core/FrameworkNumberFormat.cs, ValueConverter.FormatValue: axis
  labels, keywords, tooltips). .NET Core 3.0 writes negative zero as "-0" (an axis from -0 labelled so, the
  plot moved for its width), "G" as the shortest round-trip text (0.30000000000000004 for a step of 0.1 * 3)
  and rounds midpoints to even on the exact binary value ($0.12 for 0.125). .NET Framework: zero, 15
  significant digits, then the format's precision away from zero, which a decimal does (from 1E-28 to 7.9E+28;
  beyond, the double as it is).
- ChartHttpHandler on Linux: its keys and folders were joined with '\', no separator there (the images of
  storage=file written as "/app\charts_0\..." names in the application's folder and not found again). The
  platform's separator; '/' of a key and the url's MapPath made so.
- ChartHttpHandler read an image file with one Read (CA2022: a partial read served part of it): ReadAtLeast.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
